ndaram and the father of Ethirimnna Cinkam was killed.", "newValue": "Ethirimanna Cinkam, who reigned from 1591 to 1617, was the penultimate monarch of the Aryacakravarti dynasty, which had ruled the Jaffna Kingdom in northern Sri Lanka for over two centuries. His ascent to the throne was a direct consequence of the second Portuguese expedition to Jaffna in 1591, led by André Furtado de Mendonça, which resulted in the violent overthrow and execution of his father, King Puviraja Pandaram. Ethirimanna Cinkam's reign thus began under the shadow of Portuguese suzerainty, marking a critical transition period for the ancient Tamil kingdom.

During his rule, Ethirimanna Cinkam navigated a complex geopolitical landscape, attempting to preserve a semblance of autonomy for his kingdom while acknowledging Portuguese dominance. He was compelled to pay an annual tribute of elephants to the Portuguese crown and permit the establishment of Catholic missions, concessions that gradually eroded the kingdom's independence. Despite these constraints, he maintained a degree of internal control, oversaw administrative functions, and sought to mitigate the impact of foreign intervention on his populace, often engaging in delicate diplomatic maneuvers with both the Portuguese and other regional powers.

Ethirimanna Cinkam's reign effectively served as a protracted prelude to the complete annexation of the Jaffna Kingdom by the Portuguese. His death in 1617, without a clear successor, plunged the kingdom into a succession crisis that the Portuguese exploited to install a puppet ruler, Cankili II, before ultimately dissolving the kingdom entirely in 1619. This marked the definitive end of the Aryacakravarti dynasty and an independent Jaffna, ushering in an era of direct colonial administration that reshaped the region's political, religious, and social fabric for centuries."}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-21T01:14:44Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#44848467449", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "Classical"}, {"title": "Dynastic succession and sovereign authority traditions", "type": "Idea", "year": ""}], "newValue": ["The Portuguese Empire's aggressive expansionist policies in Asia aimed to secure strategic trading posts and convert local populations to Catholicism.", "The 1591 Portuguese expedition to Jaffna, led by André Furtado de Mendonça, resulted in the assassination of King Puviraja Pandaram, creating a power vacuum.", "Internal factionalism and the perceived weakness of the Jaffna Kingdom provided an opportune moment for foreign intervention and the imposition of suzerainty."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-21T01:14:44Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#44848467449", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Established dynastic legacies and territorial boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": "1617"}, {"title": "Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks shaping subsequent civilizati", "type": "Movement", "year": "1617"}], "newValue": ["Ethirimanna Cinkam's reign solidified Portuguese influence over the Jaffna Kingdom through tribute payments and the establishment of Catho